00:08:44.600 --> 00:08:46.640
knows what day after tomorrow.

00:08:46.640 --> 00:08:48.080
And these are the
services that we

00:08:48.080 --> 00:08:52.640
provide from digital services
and interpretation, which

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:55.460
is probably a growing
field, training data

00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:57.500
for machine learning.

00:08:57.500 --> 00:09:00.730
So here we are, and this is
what we're going to talk about,

00:09:00.730 --> 00:09:03.190
glossary case study
where we're going

00:09:03.190 --> 00:09:07.720
to compare Google
pre-trained models, which

00:09:07.720 --> 00:09:12.370
would be generic models, versus
Google pre-trained models

00:09:12.370 --> 00:09:14.380
with customized glossary.

00:09:14.380 --> 00:09:17.590
And then we're going to
compare AutoML models, which

00:09:17.590 --> 00:09:20.170
are trained with
customer corpora

00:09:20.170 --> 00:09:25.720
with a glossary put on
top of custom models.

00:09:25.720 --> 00:09:28.120
And what the glossary
would do for us

00:09:28.120 --> 00:09:32.980
is allow us to add even
more granularity on top

00:09:32.980 --> 00:09:34.960
of terminology selection.

00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:38.200
And then the languages, why
did we pick the languages?

00:09:38.200 --> 00:09:41.540
As you can see, we
have a romance group,

00:09:41.540 --> 00:09:45.610
then we have Germanic group,
then we have a Slavic group,

00:09:45.610 --> 00:09:47.470
and then we have some
more representation

00:09:47.470 --> 00:09:49.450
from the Romans
group of languages,

00:09:49.450 --> 00:09:51.500
and then we have
an Asian language.

00:09:51.500 --> 00:09:53.980
So we basically see what
the glossary feature,

00:09:53.980 --> 00:09:57.580
what the dictionary feature does
for different language groups.

00:09:57.580 --> 00:10:00.910
So obviously, we'll see highly
morphological languages,

00:10:00.910 --> 00:10:03.260
slightly less
morphological languages,

00:10:03.260 --> 00:10:07.030
and then we see Asian
languages, which basically

00:10:07.030 --> 00:10:09.920
operate on the idiograph level.

00:10:09.920 --> 00:10:12.170
So how did we rank
the languages?

00:10:12.170 --> 00:10:13.880
How did we evaluate them?

00:10:13.880 --> 00:10:17.350
We have highly
trained evaluators

00:10:17.350 --> 00:10:21.070
that used evaluation for
adequacy and fluency,

00:10:21.070 --> 00:10:24.310
and then engine
ranking, AB ranking

00:10:24.310 --> 00:10:26.620
based on error typology.

00:10:26.620 --> 00:10:32.050
And that's a methodology some
industry standard methodology

00:10:32.050 --> 00:10:35.200
combined with the localized
proprietary methodology.

00:10:35.200 --> 00:10:39.950
And then we see better
quality and user satisfaction,

00:10:39.950 --> 00:10:42.410
which in our case we're
a services providers,

00:10:42.410 --> 00:10:45.550
so it translates
into higher quality,

00:10:45.550 --> 00:10:47.990
and subsequently,
higher productivity,

00:10:47.990 --> 00:10:52.900
which in turn translates
for pricing, time to market,

00:10:52.900 --> 00:10:58.070
and better final output
quality for our customers.

00:10:58.070 --> 00:11:00.490
So why customize a glossary?

00:11:00.490 --> 00:11:01.600
Have a look.

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:05.550
Driver, driver, and driver.

00:11:05.550 --> 00:11:08.010
We provide services, as I
said, we provide services

00:11:08.010 --> 00:11:09.780
across a variety of domains.

00:11:09.780 --> 00:11:14.040
So do we want driver two
when we provide services

00:11:14.040 --> 00:11:16.470
to a hardware, software company?

00:11:16.470 --> 00:11:19.350
Obviously not.

00:11:19.350 --> 00:11:20.130
Have a look.

00:11:20.130 --> 00:11:22.830
Pin, pin, pin.

00:11:22.830 --> 00:11:25.590
Again, services across
a variety of domains.

00:11:25.590 --> 00:11:29.040
Do you want pin one,
pin two, and pin three,

00:11:29.040 --> 00:11:30.360
depending on the domain?

00:11:30.360 --> 00:11:32.310
Obviously not.

00:11:32.310 --> 00:11:35.090
Now, let's have a
look at the models.

00:11:35.090 --> 00:11:38.720
See how-- and that's
Google pre-train model

00:11:38.720 --> 00:11:41.400
with a dictionary
injected on top of it.

00:11:41.400 --> 00:11:47.030
As you can see, dictionary
pre-trained models with a--

00:11:47.030 --> 00:11:49.220
pre-trained model
without a dictionary

00:11:49.220 --> 00:11:52.880
consistently loses
to pre-trained model

00:11:52.880 --> 00:11:54.380
with a dictionary.

00:11:54.380 --> 00:11:58.400
And you can see that's
consistent across all

00:11:58.400 --> 00:12:00.290
the languages that were ranked.

00:12:00.290 --> 00:12:03.500
Maybe the difference
is not as drastic,

00:12:03.500 --> 00:12:07.340
but the difference is
consistently there.

00:12:07.340 --> 00:12:10.430
And same goes for
the engine ranking

00:12:10.430 --> 00:12:14.120
across the variety
of error types,

00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:16.820
especially with the
terminology when

00:12:16.820 --> 00:12:20.540
it comes to both in domain
terminology and customer

00:12:20.540 --> 00:12:22.280
specific terminology.

00:12:22.280 --> 00:12:25.880
And when we poll our
translators, what bothers them

00:12:25.880 --> 00:12:29.750
most as they perform
post-editing process,

00:12:29.750 --> 00:12:32.720
terminology is usually
the biggest culprit,

00:12:32.720 --> 00:12:34.790
because when the
translator translates,

00:12:34.790 --> 00:12:37.400
and what the customers
are most sensitive to,

00:12:37.400 --> 00:12:41.350
that's usually terminology.

00:12:41.350 --> 00:12:46.130
Higher number when it comes
to engine ranking for Google

00:12:46.130 --> 00:12:47.650
pre-trained models.

00:12:47.650 --> 00:12:51.370
As you can see,
higher number means

00:12:51.370 --> 00:12:56.110
which translator is better
with the following error types.

00:12:56.110 --> 00:12:57.580
Again, you can see
that the higher

00:12:57.580 --> 00:12:59.660
number means fewer errors.

00:12:59.660 --> 00:13:03.010
And again, you see a pretty
consistent picture here.

00:13:03.010 --> 00:13:08.620
Accuracy and fluency results,
now you look at Google AutoML.

00:13:08.620 --> 00:13:12.310
Google AutoML rated for
accuracy and fluency.

00:13:12.310 --> 00:13:16.420
And again, you see the
same consistent picture.

00:13:16.420 --> 00:13:19.860
So engine ranking
for Google AutoML,

00:13:19.860 --> 00:13:22.190
again, you see the same results.

00:13:22.190 --> 00:13:24.580
Whenever the
dictionary is injected,

00:13:24.580 --> 00:13:26.770
the translator preference
and the ranking

00:13:26.770 --> 00:13:30.078
is, again, in favor
of dictionary.

00:13:32.710 --> 00:13:35.020
So the summary and next steps.

00:13:35.020 --> 00:13:39.970
We obviously can see that the
gains in accuracy and fluency

00:13:39.970 --> 00:13:44.080
for all the languages
using the glossary feature

00:13:44.080 --> 00:13:49.660
comes on top, both on Google
with pre-trained models

00:13:49.660 --> 00:13:54.040
and on Google with AutoML.

00:13:54.040 --> 00:13:57.520
So the dictionary injection,
what does it do in reality?

00:13:57.520 --> 00:13:59.830
Whenever you inject
the dictionary,

00:13:59.830 --> 00:14:02.710
you refine the
terminology, whether you

00:14:02.710 --> 00:14:05.170
are using Google
pre-trained models

00:14:05.170 --> 00:14:10.560
or whether you are
using Google AutoML,

00:14:10.560 --> 00:14:13.120
and whether you are able
to refine the terminology.

00:14:13.120 --> 00:14:18.430
When you use Google AutoML, yes,
you train with customer data.

00:14:18.430 --> 00:14:21.970
But still the training data
can have some ambiguity.

00:14:21.970 --> 00:14:25.480
Some translation segments
can translate in certain way,

00:14:25.480 --> 00:14:28.480
some translation segments
can translate differently.

00:14:28.480 --> 00:14:30.280
But the dictionary
feature is very

00:14:30.280 --> 00:14:32.260
specific around the terminology.

00:14:32.260 --> 00:14:35.650
Now, a couple of things of
what we're doing at WeLocalize.

00:14:35.650 --> 00:14:38.740
We still make sure that
we [? cleanse ?] for named

00:14:38.740 --> 00:14:42.790
entities, such as proper
names, such as product names,

00:14:42.790 --> 00:14:47.500
such as geographical names,
such as, I don't know,

00:14:47.500 --> 00:14:49.030
location names.

00:14:49.030 --> 00:14:50.650
And then we also
make sure that we're

00:14:50.650 --> 00:14:54.670
very consistent around
specifying do not translate,

00:14:54.670 --> 00:14:57.590
and we remove all
terminology ambiguity.

00:14:57.590 --> 00:15:00.700
So it's also very important how
you massage your glossaries,

00:15:00.700 --> 00:15:03.160
how you massage your
dictionaries before you

00:15:03.160 --> 00:15:05.830
feed them into the Google
dictionary feature.

00:15:05.830 --> 00:15:11.590
And then next thing, we never
just utilize AutoML or Google

00:15:11.590 --> 00:15:13.060
pre-trained models.

00:15:13.060 --> 00:15:16.180
They're just by
themselves in isolation.

00:15:16.180 --> 00:15:18.370
