for two hours is a bad thing.

00:24:48.490 --> 00:24:50.170
Let's try to apply
AutoML sentiment

00:24:50.170 --> 00:24:52.210
analysis to make this better.

00:24:52.210 --> 00:24:55.810
Now, I found a data
set on CrowdFlower

00:24:55.810 --> 00:24:59.240
that is specific
to airline tweets.

00:24:59.240 --> 00:25:02.210
Multiple airlines are listed,
and this is a custom sentiment

00:25:02.210 --> 00:25:05.750
score from zero to two, zero
being the most negative,

00:25:05.750 --> 00:25:08.720
and two being the most positive.

00:25:08.720 --> 00:25:14.270
So I've taken this data set and
pointed a link to a GPS bucket

00:25:14.270 --> 00:25:16.910
with that data set on
AutoML Natural Language.

00:25:16.910 --> 00:25:20.240
All I need to do is specify that
this is the customer sentiment

00:25:20.240 --> 00:25:22.660
scale that I'm going to be
supplying AutoML Natural

00:25:22.660 --> 00:25:25.670
Language, zero to two.

00:25:25.670 --> 00:25:30.840
Quickly verify that my label's
in order, and click on Train.

00:25:30.840 --> 00:25:32.850
Now within a matter
of three hours,

00:25:32.850 --> 00:25:36.990
I get a customer sentiment
model, no code involved.

00:25:36.990 --> 00:25:38.850
All I need to do is
check the precision

00:25:38.850 --> 00:25:41.250
and recall, look at
a confusion matrix,

00:25:41.250 --> 00:25:45.720
check if my sentiment model is
confused on all of these three

00:25:45.720 --> 00:25:49.840
labels, and go back tune
my training data if needed.

00:25:49.840 --> 00:25:53.330
Look at the false
positives and negatives.

00:25:53.330 --> 00:25:56.990
Now, as you can see, our
AutoML Sentiment model,

00:25:56.990 --> 00:25:59.990
our airline sentiment
model, knows that more leg

00:25:59.990 --> 00:26:01.970
room is definitely good.

00:26:01.970 --> 00:26:04.730
And losing luggage
is not that fun.

00:26:04.730 --> 00:26:08.990
And waiting on the tarmac is
probably not that fun either.

00:26:08.990 --> 00:26:11.540
So that's sentiment
analysis for you.

00:26:11.540 --> 00:26:15.260
Our third AutoML Natural
Language model, customer entity

00:26:15.260 --> 00:26:18.650
extraction, enables
you to identify

00:26:18.650 --> 00:26:22.050
domain-specific entities
within your document.

00:26:22.050 --> 00:26:26.690
Now, why would
domain-specific entities

00:26:26.690 --> 00:26:28.970
be relevant to your business?

00:26:28.970 --> 00:26:31.520
Imagine you have a
document or you're

00:26:31.520 --> 00:26:34.880
an energy company with oil
and gas reservoir parameters

00:26:34.880 --> 00:26:37.310
that you would like to extract
from within your documents.

00:26:37.310 --> 00:26:40.410
Our regular entity
analysis API would not be--

00:26:40.410 --> 00:26:43.000
is tuned mostly for
detecting web-based entities,

00:26:43.000 --> 00:26:46.280
so it may not perform really
well in detecting, let's say,

00:26:46.280 --> 00:26:49.070
the density, the
pressure, viscosity values

00:26:49.070 --> 00:26:52.630
of a certain reservoir
in the Gulf of Mexico.

00:26:52.630 --> 00:26:54.397
Let's say you have
doctor's patient notes

00:26:54.397 --> 00:26:55.980
that you are trying
to analyze and you

00:26:55.980 --> 00:26:59.230
would like to extract
specific symptoms or diseases

00:26:59.230 --> 00:27:03.250
from within that patient note.

00:27:03.250 --> 00:27:07.450
You could be-- and well, the
invoice example that we just

00:27:07.450 --> 00:27:12.193
saw, we are able to predict,
well, prices, numbers, phone

00:27:12.193 --> 00:27:13.360
numbers, so on and so forth.

00:27:13.360 --> 00:27:16.600
But they may be specific dates,
like billing dates, supplier

00:27:16.600 --> 00:27:17.222
dates, so on.

00:27:17.222 --> 00:27:21.220
And so there is variations of
entities within those documents

00:27:21.220 --> 00:27:25.220
that you would like to tune
and tag on those documents.

00:27:25.220 --> 00:27:29.500
AutoML entity extraction
enables you to do just that

00:27:29.500 --> 00:27:30.790
without any code involved.

00:27:30.790 --> 00:27:34.210
Again, all you'd have to do
is provide some examples,

00:27:34.210 --> 00:27:38.110
and we ask for at least 10
mentions of each entity type--

00:27:38.110 --> 00:27:40.060
100 for a good model--

00:27:40.060 --> 00:27:43.300
so that you can start building
a custom entity extraction

00:27:43.300 --> 00:27:46.870
API that performs really
well for your own business

00:27:46.870 --> 00:27:51.780
specific use cases, or your
own domain-specific documents.

00:27:51.780 --> 00:27:53.290
Let's launch into a quick demo.

