00:28:52.760 --> 00:28:55.700
we want to extract.

00:28:55.700 --> 00:28:58.510
AutoML Natural Language expects
to read our JSON L files

00:28:58.510 --> 00:29:00.960
from a Google Cloud bucket.

00:29:00.960 --> 00:29:02.760
In addition to our
JSON L data, we'll

00:29:02.760 --> 00:29:05.220
make a CSV file that tells
it where in Google Cloud

00:29:05.220 --> 00:29:07.210
we've stored our data.

00:29:07.210 --> 00:29:08.950
Here we specify which
data file should

00:29:08.950 --> 00:29:10.630
be used for
training, validation,

00:29:10.630 --> 00:29:12.970
and testing of our model.

00:29:12.970 --> 00:29:15.360
If you don't break your data
set into these three files,

00:29:15.360 --> 00:29:19.290
AutoML Natural Language will
do it for you automatically.

00:29:19.290 --> 00:29:21.390
Now let's upload our data.

00:29:21.390 --> 00:29:23.700
Here in the AutoML natural
language interface,

00:29:23.700 --> 00:29:25.880
we'll create a new data set.

00:29:25.880 --> 00:29:28.820
To import the data, we give
AutoML Natural Language the CSV

00:29:28.820 --> 00:29:31.900
file we made, and then we wait.

00:29:31.900 --> 00:29:34.150
Importing data can take a
couple of minutes to finish,

00:29:34.150 --> 00:29:37.190
but you'll get an
email when it's done.

00:29:37.190 --> 00:29:40.010
Once our data is imported, we
can view individual examples

00:29:40.010 --> 00:29:42.957
to make sure they were
labeled correctly.

00:29:42.957 --> 00:29:45.290
We can check where in the
text different custom entities

00:29:45.290 --> 00:29:46.850
were annotated.

00:29:46.850 --> 00:29:48.650
We can also annotate
a new entity right

00:29:48.650 --> 00:29:51.893
here from within a tool.

00:29:51.893 --> 00:29:54.060
Notice that we can also
tell how many examples we've

00:29:54.060 --> 00:29:55.560
got for each label.

00:29:55.560 --> 00:29:57.660
We can begin training a
model by navigating over

00:29:57.660 --> 00:30:00.550
to the Train tab and
clicking Start Training.

00:30:00.550 --> 00:30:02.860
Training a model can
take up to three hours.

00:30:02.860 --> 00:30:06.190
Behind the scenes, AutoML will
do a neural architecture search

00:30:06.190 --> 00:30:08.200
to find the best model it can.

00:30:08.200 --> 00:30:10.430
So now's a good time
to take a coffee break.

00:30:10.430 --> 00:30:13.117
You'll get an email when the
model's finished training.

00:30:13.117 --> 00:30:15.700
When training is finished, we'll
be able to see our model here

00:30:15.700 --> 00:30:17.178
in the Train tab.

00:30:17.178 --> 00:30:18.970
We can see how well it
did by clicking here

00:30:18.970 --> 00:30:21.770
to see full evaluation.

00:30:21.770 --> 00:30:23.420
Here in the evaluate
panel, we can

00:30:23.420 --> 00:30:26.330
see the performance of our
model on different labels.

00:30:26.330 --> 00:30:29.700
The metrics we'll want to look
at are precision and recall.

00:30:29.700 --> 00:30:31.860
Precision tells us of
the snips the model

00:30:31.860 --> 00:30:35.600
labeled applicant, how many
were really applicants.

00:30:35.600 --> 00:30:38.660
Recall tells us of all the real
applicants in our data set,

00:30:38.660 --> 00:30:42.357
what percent were accurately
recognized by the model.

00:30:42.357 --> 00:30:44.690
We can also scroll down to
see where the model correctly

00:30:44.690 --> 00:30:47.420
labeled applicant-- these
are the true positives--

00:30:47.420 --> 00:30:50.210
and where it accidentally
mislabeled text as applicant,

00:30:50.210 --> 00:30:52.390
or false positives.

00:30:52.390 --> 00:30:54.760
In the Test and Use tab,
we can start actually

00:30:54.760 --> 00:30:57.273
using our model
hosted by Google.

00:30:57.273 --> 00:30:59.440
To do that, we first have
to deploy our model, which

00:30:59.440 --> 00:31:01.470
could take a few minutes.

00:31:01.470 --> 00:31:04.120
Next, we can try to extract
custom entities on a new text

00:31:04.120 --> 00:31:06.460
snippet.

00:31:06.460 --> 00:31:07.420
There we go.

00:31:07.420 --> 00:31:10.060
Here are the custom
entities we extracted.

00:31:10.060 --> 00:31:11.100
That's all for now.

00:31:11.100 --> 00:31:12.812
Have fun modeling.

00:31:12.812 --> 00:31:16.620
