00:33:08.980 --> 00:33:10.160
they will share similar--

00:33:10.160 --> 00:33:11.420
I love DocuSign stories.

00:33:11.420 --> 00:33:13.370
And it is such a
great honor to work

00:33:13.370 --> 00:33:16.910
for a product that meaningfully
touches people's life, and such

00:33:16.910 --> 00:33:21.200
a huge impact, as they are
dealing with important moments

00:33:21.200 --> 00:33:23.290
and issues of their life.

00:33:23.290 --> 00:33:24.640
So that's us.

00:33:24.640 --> 00:33:27.240
That's who we are at DocuSign.

00:33:27.240 --> 00:33:32.530
Now, let me talk about the
problem we're trying to solve.

00:33:32.530 --> 00:33:35.140
Today, when our
customers send documents

00:33:35.140 --> 00:33:38.580
out for signing using
our web or mobile app,

00:33:38.580 --> 00:33:40.920
they had to manually place tags.

00:33:40.920 --> 00:33:41.740
So what is tagging?

00:33:41.740 --> 00:33:43.650
What does this word tag mean?

00:33:43.650 --> 00:33:48.220
Tag is a DocuSign term used
for identifying form input

00:33:48.220 --> 00:33:52.930
fields where customers
can input data, and also

00:33:52.930 --> 00:33:54.460
draw the signatures.

00:33:54.460 --> 00:33:56.060
So let's take an example.

00:33:56.060 --> 00:33:58.690
There's a NDA document that
I sent out for signing.

00:33:58.690 --> 00:34:00.400
You'd expect your
customer to input

00:34:00.400 --> 00:34:03.070
the name, the date they
signed the document,

00:34:03.070 --> 00:34:04.330
the signature itself.

00:34:04.330 --> 00:34:06.880
All these different input
fields are for those tags.

00:34:06.880 --> 00:34:10.010
And as we can see on
the animated GIF there,

00:34:10.010 --> 00:34:11.790
manual tagging can be done.

00:34:11.790 --> 00:34:13.010
We do it today.

00:34:13.010 --> 00:34:17.300
But it does delay time to
value, and it is error prone.

00:34:17.300 --> 00:34:19.489
Our goal is to
seamlessly identify

00:34:19.489 --> 00:34:22.969
tags based on different
tag types in categories,

00:34:22.969 --> 00:34:26.630
and place them on the document
so that user doesn't have

00:34:26.630 --> 00:34:30.139
to place a tag, move it around,
make sure it's properly x

00:34:30.139 --> 00:34:33.350
and y coordinated, and
it is matching the label

00:34:33.350 --> 00:34:34.699
for which the tag belongs to.

00:34:37.409 --> 00:34:40.699
So when we thought about solving
this problem, and why Kiran,

00:34:40.699 --> 00:34:42.929
why did mobile pick
up this problem?

00:34:42.929 --> 00:34:47.430
Imagine tagging or placing
tags on this form factor.

00:34:47.430 --> 00:34:48.750
It is not fun.

00:34:48.750 --> 00:34:51.980
We definitely wanted
to make this process--

00:34:51.980 --> 00:34:54.960
for my domain, it
was a big problem.

00:34:54.960 --> 00:34:57.260
And I tried many things.

00:34:57.260 --> 00:34:58.460
We tried OCR.

00:34:58.460 --> 00:35:00.680
We tried OCR with custom code.

00:35:00.680 --> 00:35:02.450
None of them really
worked at the scale

00:35:02.450 --> 00:35:04.160
at which DocuSign operates.

00:35:04.160 --> 00:35:06.350
And the different use
cases my customers had.

00:35:06.350 --> 00:35:09.337
All the different document types
I had, none of them really,

00:35:09.337 --> 00:35:09.920
really worked.

00:35:09.920 --> 00:35:14.380
So the only way to do this right
was using AI machine learning.

00:35:14.380 --> 00:35:17.860
So we partnered with Google,
and the first question

00:35:17.860 --> 00:35:18.820
was why Google?

00:35:18.820 --> 00:35:21.230
I think there are three key
reasons I call them out.

00:35:21.230 --> 00:35:25.000
One, I believe Google has
the best-in-class product

00:35:25.000 --> 00:35:30.340
for Vision deep learning
AI, and Natural Language AI.

00:35:30.340 --> 00:35:32.990
I do think that they have
best-in-class products there.

00:35:32.990 --> 00:35:36.340
The second key factor is AutoML
is a very powerful tool which

00:35:36.340 --> 00:35:40.040
helps us manage our model
accuracies and performance.

00:35:40.040 --> 00:35:42.670
And finally, partner with Google
engineering and [INAUDIBLE]

00:35:42.670 --> 00:35:43.378
was a lot of fun.

00:35:43.378 --> 00:35:45.212
Being an engineer myself,
I had a lot of fun

00:35:45.212 --> 00:35:47.560
working with Google team,
partnering with them, because

00:35:47.560 --> 00:35:51.263
on a scale of solution, solving
this problem could not be done,

00:35:51.263 --> 00:35:52.930
in the [? first written ?]
by ourselves.

00:35:52.930 --> 00:35:54.820
We needed some help there.

00:35:54.820 --> 00:35:57.035
So essentially we had
three different models,

00:35:57.035 --> 00:35:58.410
and I'm going to
walk you through

00:35:58.410 --> 00:36:00.952
the different architecture, how
we solved this problem pretty

00:36:00.952 --> 00:36:01.870
soon.

00:36:01.870 --> 00:36:03.328
But we have three
different models.

00:36:03.328 --> 00:36:06.000
First, we are using
Vision OCR, we

00:36:06.000 --> 00:36:09.330
are using AutoML Vision
object detection model,

00:36:09.330 --> 00:36:14.130
and AutoML Natural Language
entity extraction models.

00:36:14.130 --> 00:36:15.690
And all these three
different models

00:36:15.690 --> 00:36:18.810
we trained with thousands
and thousands of documents,

00:36:18.810 --> 00:36:20.850
and hundreds and
thousands of tags.

00:36:20.850 --> 00:36:23.100
That's the only way we
could get the accuracies we

00:36:23.100 --> 00:36:26.080
are seeing right now.

00:36:26.080 --> 00:36:29.760
Before I actually talk about
the architecture, let me demo

00:36:29.760 --> 00:36:32.368
and show you guys how
this really works.

00:36:32.368 --> 00:36:33.660
Can you switch to demo, please.

00:36:37.920 --> 00:36:41.750
So here I have my mobile device,
and the first thing I'm doing

00:36:41.750 --> 00:36:45.373
is launching our DocuSign app.

00:36:45.373 --> 00:36:47.540
As you can see, there's a
Create button for somebody

00:36:47.540 --> 00:36:49.040
to create a document,
as I mentioned

00:36:49.040 --> 00:36:50.510
before, the use
case is somebody's

00:36:50.510 --> 00:36:52.590
trying to create a document.

00:36:52.590 --> 00:36:57.050
So I create a document,
I load a document first,

00:36:57.050 --> 00:36:59.675
and from my local file
directory, I'll pick a NDA.

00:37:02.750 --> 00:37:04.510
The document is loaded.

00:37:04.510 --> 00:37:06.520
In typical DocuSign
world, any NDA

00:37:06.520 --> 00:37:09.320
would require two different
people agreeing to an NDA.

00:37:09.320 --> 00:37:12.180
So we call them signers.

00:37:12.180 --> 00:37:14.770
In this case, I have two
different test users.

00:37:14.770 --> 00:37:19.960
I'll use Tamara,
my first signer.

00:37:19.960 --> 00:37:23.360
And the recipient here, in
this case, I'll use Martin.

00:37:26.283 --> 00:37:27.700
So these are my
two different test

00:37:27.700 --> 00:37:29.500
users I'm using for signing.

00:37:29.500 --> 00:37:32.590
The next thing I want to
do is load the document

00:37:32.590 --> 00:37:35.890
and send it out for signing.

00:37:35.890 --> 00:37:40.060
Now, as you can see,
in literally instantly,

00:37:40.060 --> 00:37:42.210
without user having to
do any interaction, then

00:37:42.210 --> 00:37:45.880
tag document, this tag, all
the fields I'm zooming in,

00:37:45.880 --> 00:37:46.990
all the fields are tagged.

00:37:46.990 --> 00:37:49.190
If you go look at the
bottom of the document,

00:37:49.190 --> 00:37:51.730
both the recipients
will need to sign a tag.

00:37:51.730 --> 00:37:52.990
Ready to go.

00:37:52.990 --> 00:37:58.950
And all I need to
do is long press

00:37:58.950 --> 00:38:02.640
and assign few fields to
Martin, and this document

00:38:02.640 --> 00:38:03.733
is ready to be sent.

00:38:07.360 --> 00:38:09.970
So if you can
imagine, the entire

00:38:09.970 --> 00:38:11.260
process of tagging very--

00:38:11.260 --> 00:38:13.990
I had to manually place tags,
make sure they're aligned,

00:38:13.990 --> 00:38:15.153
all that is gone.

00:38:15.153 --> 00:38:16.820
So some of you might
be thinking, Kiran,

00:38:16.820 --> 00:38:18.170
that's a simple use case.

00:38:18.170 --> 00:38:19.550
India had like 10 fields, dude.

00:38:19.550 --> 00:38:20.380
Come on, really?

00:38:20.380 --> 00:38:21.400
Is it that hard?

00:38:21.400 --> 00:38:23.530
So let's take a more
complex use case.

00:38:23.530 --> 00:38:25.700
I have another example here.

00:38:25.700 --> 00:38:30.072
So I'm using a US passport form.

00:38:30.072 --> 00:38:32.030
So I don't know how many
of you, but I recently

00:38:32.030 --> 00:38:33.520
renewed our passport form.

00:38:33.520 --> 00:38:38.330
They are at least 350 fields
on this document, if not more.

00:38:38.330 --> 00:38:41.760
So I'm going to take a US
passport form, load it.

00:38:41.760 --> 00:38:44.113
For the sake of
using a user, I'm

00:38:44.113 --> 00:38:45.405
going to continue using Tamara.

00:38:48.130 --> 00:38:52.670
And now I kick-off tagging.

00:38:56.100 --> 00:38:58.620
And you can see, now
this is instantaneous.

00:38:58.620 --> 00:39:00.680
Then tag document is tagged.

00:39:00.680 --> 00:39:03.160
The date, the check boxes.

00:39:03.160 --> 00:39:06.220
Now let's go through
the rest of the fields.

00:39:06.220 --> 00:39:07.150
There it goes, guys.

00:39:07.150 --> 00:39:09.190
Look at the beauty of this.

00:39:09.190 --> 00:39:12.610
All these fields are tagged,
the signature tag is tagged,

00:39:12.610 --> 00:39:16.110
the whole document is
tagged and ready to be sent.

00:39:18.650 --> 00:39:19.965
Back to the slides, please.

00:39:24.820 --> 00:39:28.773
So as simple as the demo was,
as quick as the demo was,

00:39:28.773 --> 00:39:30.190
that's the beauty
of auto tagging.

00:39:30.190 --> 00:39:31.940
That's the beauty of
AI machine learning.

00:39:31.940 --> 00:39:34.840
You can really
simplify and eliminate

00:39:34.840 --> 00:39:38.110
manual processes that otherwise
would have taken a lot of time

00:39:38.110 --> 00:39:40.390
for normal users to do.

00:39:40.390 --> 00:39:45.180
So I'm going to now explain how
we actually solve this problem.

00:39:45.180 --> 00:39:47.350
I'm going to start at
three different levels.

00:39:47.350 --> 00:39:49.650
At the document level,
which is simple business--

00:39:49.650 --> 00:39:51.780
for a business owner,
what does it mean?

00:39:51.780 --> 00:39:53.988
And then I'm going to talk
about architecture and how

00:39:53.988 --> 00:39:55.790
the models really work.

00:39:55.790 --> 00:39:58.070
At the core document level,
let's take an example

00:39:58.070 --> 00:39:59.960
I'm loading a W-9 form.

00:39:59.960 --> 00:40:02.440
I want to use this for tagging.

00:40:02.440 --> 00:40:07.000
The first thing we do is extract
text, the OCR aspect of, hey,

00:40:07.000 --> 00:40:09.740
what are the different text
components in this document?

00:40:09.740 --> 00:40:12.760
The next thing I do is
identify bounding boxes.

00:40:12.760 --> 00:40:15.310
For people who have done
machine learning on Vision,

00:40:15.310 --> 00:40:19.750
they know bounding boxes is
the technical machine learning

00:40:19.750 --> 00:40:20.500
term for tags.

00:40:20.500 --> 00:40:22.125
And sorry to throw
another term on you,

00:40:22.125 --> 00:40:25.030
but they're called
as bounding boxes.

00:40:25.030 --> 00:40:27.730
Once we get those results
back from both of them,

00:40:27.730 --> 00:40:29.530
we process them to
form fill analysis

00:40:29.530 --> 00:40:34.060
where we apply more contextual
meaning of the bounding box

00:40:34.060 --> 00:40:34.880
with the document.

00:40:34.880 --> 00:40:37.510
For example, if
there's a sign here

00:40:37.510 --> 00:40:40.000
and there's a tag next to
it, it is a signature tag.

00:40:40.000 --> 00:40:41.200
It's not a text field.

00:40:41.200 --> 00:40:45.130
So applying that concept is
the form fill analysis we do.

00:40:45.130 --> 00:40:47.885
And then eventually, as you see
on the screenshot, by the way,

00:40:47.885 --> 00:40:49.510
that's coming out
from our auto tagging

00:40:49.510 --> 00:40:50.900
machine learning algorithms.

00:40:50.900 --> 00:40:54.070
So there's a completely tagged
W-9 form there to be used.

00:40:57.460 --> 00:40:59.960
I'm going to go a level deeper,
talk about how it's actually

00:40:59.960 --> 00:41:02.120
architected.

00:41:02.120 --> 00:41:05.030
A user makes a call
to a GCP endpoint.

00:41:05.030 --> 00:41:08.480
We internally call
an App Engine.

00:41:08.480 --> 00:41:11.470
We need App Engine because
I'm not making a straight call

00:41:11.470 --> 00:41:12.530
to single model.

00:41:12.530 --> 00:41:15.190
We actually have multiple
models who do different work.

00:41:15.190 --> 00:41:16.940
So we want to do some
orchestration there,

00:41:16.940 --> 00:41:19.930
and App Engine helps us
do that orchestration.

00:41:19.930 --> 00:41:22.210
The first call we make
is a simultaneous call

00:41:22.210 --> 00:41:29.110
to OCR Vision model, and AutoML
Vision object detection model.

00:41:29.110 --> 00:41:30.830
Then the idea of
those two models

00:41:30.830 --> 00:41:34.540
is extracting text,
identifying the bounding boxes.

00:41:34.540 --> 00:41:39.835
The results that we get back
we feed back to the App Engine.

00:41:39.835 --> 00:41:41.830
App Engine then
forwards that request

00:41:41.830 --> 00:41:44.350
to AutoML Natural
Language entity extraction

00:41:44.350 --> 00:41:48.340
model, which does form field
analysis and processing.

00:41:48.340 --> 00:41:51.515
And those results get
fed back to App Engine

00:41:51.515 --> 00:41:52.390
where we do clean up.

00:41:52.390 --> 00:41:55.950
Why we need to do clean is a
lot of times tags will overlap,

00:41:55.950 --> 00:41:57.980
if there are two tags
very close to each other.

00:41:57.980 --> 00:41:59.650
So there is a lot
of noise, and you

00:41:59.650 --> 00:42:01.233
want to eliminate
some of those noise.

00:42:01.233 --> 00:42:04.750
And so some of that logic is
built-in our App Engine site.

00:42:04.750 --> 00:42:06.800
It returns us then
back to Cloud endpoint,

00:42:06.800 --> 00:42:10.720
and finally we give the
results back to the users.

00:42:10.720 --> 00:42:16.870
So it looks a little complicated
because for the scale DocuSign

00:42:16.870 --> 00:42:21.010
operates, we operate at billions
and billions of documents

00:42:21.010 --> 00:42:22.180
and transactions.

00:42:22.180 --> 00:42:26.500
We had to use multiple models
to come back and provide

00:42:26.500 --> 00:42:29.550
the proper results that
you saw in the demo.

00:42:29.550 --> 00:42:31.940
Now let's talk about how do
the models really operate.

00:42:31.940 --> 00:42:35.080
So now this is how document flow
works, how the architecture.

00:42:35.080 --> 00:42:37.940
Well, the next step is
understanding how models work.

00:42:37.940 --> 00:42:39.980
Again, this is a
very simplistic view.

00:42:39.980 --> 00:42:42.380
Internally, the models
are more complex, but just

00:42:42.380 --> 00:42:44.430
for sake of understanding.

00:42:44.430 --> 00:42:47.360
Let's say the Vision
object detection,

00:42:47.360 --> 00:42:50.000
at your right at the very
top, identified something

00:42:50.000 --> 00:42:51.120
as a text tag.

00:42:51.120 --> 00:42:53.570
So the category you're
trying to find, hey,

00:42:53.570 --> 00:42:56.090
I looked at the document, it
looks like it's a text tag.

00:42:56.090 --> 00:42:59.090
And the OCR model found,
hey, this text tag

00:42:59.090 --> 00:43:02.370
is between words saying,
sign in favor of.

00:43:02.370 --> 00:43:03.290
OK?

00:43:03.290 --> 00:43:07.220
Now, when we mark the
results, you see sign,

00:43:07.220 --> 00:43:10.360
there's a text tab, and
there's sign in favor of.

00:43:10.360 --> 00:43:12.900
So this tag actually
is a signature tag.

00:43:12.900 --> 00:43:16.160
So Natural Language
entity extraction

00:43:16.160 --> 00:43:20.600
classifies bounding
boxes and represents them

00:43:20.600 --> 00:43:23.250
in the context of document text.

00:43:23.250 --> 00:43:27.360
And now the result is you can
see the category of the tag

00:43:27.360 --> 00:43:29.200
is signature.

00:43:29.200 --> 00:43:32.980
So that's the beauty of Natural
Language entity extraction

00:43:32.980 --> 00:43:34.500
models.

00:43:34.500 --> 00:43:39.300
So well, implementation of
this is a lot more complicated.

00:43:39.300 --> 00:43:41.140
It does take more time.

00:43:41.140 --> 00:43:44.160
But I'm going to take a
step back and talk about

00:43:44.160 --> 00:43:46.020
what does it mean for
users point of view?

00:43:46.020 --> 00:43:48.040
What does it mean
for the business?

00:43:48.040 --> 00:43:50.190
We actually solve
three problems.

00:43:50.190 --> 00:43:54.390
At the very foundation, we
are making agreement processes

00:43:54.390 --> 00:43:55.310
faster.

00:43:55.310 --> 00:43:57.180
We are eliminating manual steps.

00:43:57.180 --> 00:44:00.570
It becomes easier
for customers to use.

00:44:00.570 --> 00:44:04.130
Secondly, once you apply
machine learning logic,

00:44:04.130 --> 00:44:05.660
it's less error prone.

00:44:05.660 --> 00:44:09.420
It is a lot more user friendly.

00:44:09.420 --> 00:44:11.750
There's more at option,
so it's more reliable.

00:44:11.750 --> 00:44:13.940
And like any other
machine learning project,

00:44:13.940 --> 00:44:15.440
it is self-learning
and intelligent.

00:44:15.440 --> 00:44:19.100
As these models are used more,
as these models are seeing more

00:44:19.100 --> 00:44:20.780
data and get trained
more, they just

00:44:20.780 --> 00:44:23.290
keep getting better over time.

00:44:23.290 --> 00:44:27.340
So in business view, we
are making a process fast,

00:44:27.340 --> 00:44:30.350
reliable, and intelligent.

00:44:30.350 --> 00:44:35.090
Finally, that's pretty much
how DocuSign solved our problem

00:44:35.090 --> 00:44:36.160
with the help of Google.

